ight to  complain  which is valid  but my perspective is that  wikipedia provides a service  i was a user i am a user i appreciate  wikipedia and i was prepared to be a  contributor  and i was contributing content which was  in essence the same as content they have  on other language systems i maybe didn't  get it right  they could have communicated with me and  said no that's not quite how we like it  please do this instead of just deleting  it  at link  lots of people have trouble with link  everybody who asks us whether they're  paying members or free members we take  the trouble to explain things to them  and i think any organization whether  they're for-profit not-for-profit free  they're all in the same business they're  in the a the business of providing a  service and b they hope to grow and  prosper as an organism as an  organization i make no distinctions  between for-profit not-for-profit or  anything else it's all human endeavor  uh human beings  a like to be successful in whatever  they're doing in whatever their project  is and human beings like to be treated  politely at any rate the debate raged i  was told that i couldn't accept  criticism i couldn't accept when i'm  wrong all that kind of stuff just  because i didn't agree with what  everyone else was saying or not everyone  but a lot of people anyway enough of  that i think we've exhausted ourselves  on that one  um  i also posted  some thoughts that i had on how link  could be used in a classroom  and i hope to get some feedback i see  val in tokyo has come back and i want to  talk to her and she has some suggestions  that my approach may not work and so i  really want to learn about how  people who are familiar with link might  use link in the classroom i've put my  ideas up simply just to get the  discussion started so what i want to  focus on today is  it has to do with with flashcards and i  had a  comment from someone who follows my  videos here  and thanked me and said that it helped  you know inspire her to learn more  languages and so forth and that she's a  student and she's very keen on languages  and she's now  deciding that she has decided she wants  to go after russian and spanish and  she's trying to do russian and she finds  it very difficult and she reviews her  flash guards both at link and in anki  and whatever hundreds of times i still  can't remember them do i have any advice  yes my advice is my advice is don't  overdo flash cards  uh i think there is far too much  emphasis placed on flash cards whether  it be space repetition systems  uh what was it called super memo anki i  mean those are all fine  as an adjunct to a language learning  program in my view now other people may  have different approaches but to me  max five percent of the time that i  spend in language learning is spent  reviewing flash cards and there are a  few reasons first of all in my russian  studies  i can't remember the numbers exactly  link tells me that i have saved 25 000  links  and i have learned 55 000 words  and  as i've said many times learned means or  that  knowing a word means that i know what it  means it doesn't necessarily mean that i  can use it  any language learner has a far larger  passive vocabulary than active  vocabulary nothing wrong with that you  still know what the word means you can  read you can listen you can understand  now when i read something new at link  the new words to me are in blue and they  largely correspond to words  either that i don't know or that i just  haven't come across before in fact i  know many of them i know most of those  words so that number 55 000 to the to  the extent that it's reflected in what i  see when i open up new content at link  is quite accurate so in three years i've  learned 55 000 words  i have saved 25 000 words there is no  way that i would have enough time  nor would i be inclined  to spend enough time  reviewing flash cards in order to learn  those words  so when you review flash cards you're  seeing the words as flash guards it's  you're seeing  you're able to focus on a few of them  relationships between different words  it helps to some extent some of them  when you go through the flash cards  you'll get them right away some of them  you will never learn you can review them  100 times you won't remember them  it's far better to spend your time  reviewing these words in their natural  setting  reading and listening particularly in  link where any word that you have saved  is now highlighted in yellow and that  helps to trigger i find my memory so i  see the yellow word i see the meaning  when i hover  i see it in a new context i'm reminded  that this is a word that i've saved  before  that's how i learned plus you pick up a  lot of words especially the way we count  words in link we count every  form of the word as a different word so  chances are when you've learned one form  of the word pretty soon you'll be  picking up the others without having to  save them  so  between reviewing the words you've  already saved when you read  and  seeing related words  guessing at them plus other words that  you pick up through context because you  now know more and more words  the main activity should be reading and  listening  and i mean i know that i spend i would  guess five percent of my time  on reviewing my flashcards i do it when  i get my flashcards by email because  typically  the uh the way we work at link when i  get my email  with with my saved links the bulk of  them are ones that i've saved recently  so they are relatively fresh in my mind  the context where i saw them is  relatively fresh  so i have a better chance to get a bit  of a head start on trying to learn them  sometimes if i'm busy and i don't review  my daily uh email of flashcards then  they've already kind of become frozen  but that's okay too because the next  time i run into them in my reading  they'll become they'll thaw out for me  you know and words that you are never  ever going to encounter again in your  listening and reading that you came  across because you need it for one  lesson and you saved it and it's now on  your flash card list and you can never  remember it and it's the type of flower  that grows in northeastern siberia or  whatever the word is  forget it it doesn't matter you won't be  able to remember them all focus on the  ones that keep on coming back spend most  of your time listening and reading and  writing and speaking and so forth and  don't overdo flash guarding whether it  be with link anki super memo or whatever  to me it is a relatively inefficient way  of learning far better to get in there  with a meaningful context get some  emotional involvement create some  context remind yourself that you've seen  the word before and you'll find that  before you know it you're able to read a  lot and you know a lot of words  so there you have it that's the end of  my uh little discussion today thank you  for listening
 

